Maybe module `opcodes` array values are all 0 because `yr_get_integer` with `opcodes[%i]` field lookup fails due array declaration? But numeric seq3 match [0x28,0x2a] would fail if all zero. It returned 1 earlier. Wait seq3 was `dotnet.opcode_sequence(0x28, 0x2a)` and output seq31. Could be because YARA resolved `(0x28,0x2a)` to **wrong overload**? There are `(ii)` and also `(ss)` etc. It chose numeric. If `opcodes` array didn't exist, helper would get zeros => no match. So some opcodes fetched correctly.

Maybe issue is `dotnet_match_from` greedy algorithm: It processes entire remaining opcode array from start, requiring pattern to match at the **beginning and consume all opcodes**? Let's examine loop: while i<opcode_count. If exact matches and j reaches pattern_count, then next iteration: j==pattern_count, no gap, gap_pattern maybe -1 -> returns false unless i==opcode_count at end. So it only returns true if pattern consumes the **entire remaining suffix**! For numeric seq3, it matched because there is a method whose opcode suffix exactly [0x28,0x2a] (Application_BeginQuit has exactly call,ret). For [0x02] there is no method consisting only of ldarg.0. So bug: should return true when j == pattern_count regardless of remaining opcodes. At top of while, check `if (j == pattern_count) return true;` unless remaining can only be gaps? Once pattern fully matched, success. Add:
```
while (i < opcode_count) {
 if (j == pattern_count) return true;
 ...
}
...
return j == pattern_count;
```
But with trailing `*` pattern? Pattern ends with gap, j may not reach m until trailing while. If pattern fully matched (j==m) yes return true. If pattern has remaining gap after exact, j<m, continue; gap consumes. Good. Add check at top. Also if opcode_count=0 handled.

Patch and remove debug.

